    <section id="guide-en" class="shell space-y-14 py-16 md:py-24" dir="ltr">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Visitor Guide · English</p>
        <h2 class="section-title mt-2">About Jinnah Park</h2>
        <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-8 text-slate-700">
          <p>Welcome to <strong>Jinnah Park</strong>, widely recognized as the central public park in Rawalpindi. Located in the heart of <strong>Rawalpindi</strong>, <strong>Punjab</strong>, <strong>Pakistan</strong>, this destination serves as a primary hub for travelers visiting the region.</p>
          <p class="font-bold text-green-900">Jinnah Park → Rawalpindi → Punjab → Pakistan</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="section-title">Location &amp; How to Visit Jinnah Park in Rawalpindi</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-8 text-slate-600">Jinnah Park is located on Mushtaq Baig Shaheed Road, Chaklala Cantt., Rawalpindi, 46000, Pakistan. It sits about 30–35 km from Islamabad International Airport (ISB), roughly a 40–50 minute drive. Visitors can reach it via the Rawalpindi–Islamabad Metrobus, ride-hailing apps (Careem, InDrive, Bykea), taxi, train (Rawalpindi Railway Station, ~5–6 km), or private car. Set your GPS to “Jinnah Park, Mushtaq Baig Shaheed Road, Chaklala Cantt.” for the most accurate route.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="section-title">Landmarks &amp; Attractions Around Jinnah Park</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-8 text-slate-600">When visiting <strong>Jinnah Park</strong>, visitors can easily explore surrounding historical landmarks and points of interest, including <strong>Ayub National Park</strong> and <strong>Pakistan Army Museum</strong>. Both lie within Rawalpindi and can be combined into a single day of sightseeing.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="section-title">History &amp; Significance of Jinnah Park</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-8 text-slate-600">The grounds of Jinnah Park were once the Central Jail Rawalpindi, established in 1882 and demolished in 1988 after prisoners were moved to Adiala Jail. The Rawalpindi Development Authority (RDA) began converting the historic site into a public park in 1995; the project was later handed to the Pakistan Army's X Corps, and the park finally opened to the public in 2006. The park is named after Muhammad Ali Jinnah, the founder of Pakistan.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-8 text-slate-500">Sources: Wikipedia (English), DAWN, and The News International public records.</p>
      </div>
